This loop trusts resolve-merge-conflicts' self-reported Judgment field
to decide whether to pause, so the same caveat applies here: nothing
independently verifies that a resolution the sub-agent called mechanical
actually was.

The DU/UD/AU/UA/DD/AA set is what git printed for every add/delete shape
I constructed and checked by hand on this machine (both directions of
modify/delete, add/add). I did not check whether copy detection,
submodule conflicts, or other configurations produce codes outside that
set.

I extracted the classifier's rationale to a reference file but left the
Step 2 pacing rewrite inline, even though it also grew the file by a
fair amount. That content seemed like something a reader needs at
execution time on every pass, not detail to look up on demand, so I
didn't push it out too.
